{"edits": [{"finding_ids": ["app/models.py:17:root-validator"], "search": "from pydantic import BaseModel, root_validator", "replace": "from pydantic import BaseModel, ConfigDict, model_validator", "explanation": "Import the v2 names used by the replacement: ConfigDict for model_config and model_validator for the after-validator. root_validator is no longer used. Per the migration guide, @root_validator is deprecated and should be replaced with @model_validator."}, {"finding_ids": ["app/models.py:17:root-validator", "app/models.py:23:config-class"], "search": "    @root_validator
    def compute_total(cls, values):
        lines = values.get("lines") or []
        values["total"] = round(sum(l.qty * l.price for l in lines), 2)
        return values

    class Config:
        validate_assignment = True", "replace": "    model_config = ConfigDict(validate_assignment=True)

    @model_validator(mode='after')
    def compute_total(self):
        lines = self.lines or []
        # Write to __dict__ directly: with validate_assignment=True, a normal
        # attribute assignment here would re-trigger this after-validator and recurse.
        self.__dict__['total'] = round(sum(l.qty * l.price for l in lines), 2)
        return self", "explanation": "Per the 'Changes to config' section, the class-based Config is deprecated and becomes model_config = ConfigDict(...) with the same key (validate_assignment) unchanged. Per the validator section, @root_validator becomes @model_validator(mode='after') taking an instance and returning self. Since the model is now an instance, values are read/written via self. The total is written through __dict__ to avoid re-entering validation under validate_assignment=True."}], "notes": "Both findings are addressed. The import edit and the class-body edit do not overlap. Behavior caveats: (1) In v1, root_validator ran on the raw values dict before field validation finished; in v2 an 'after' model validator only runs once fields are valid, so inputs that fail field validation no longer reach compute_total. This is the intended v2 semantics and is noted in the migration guide. (2) The total assignment writes to self.__dict__ instead of self.total = ..., because with validate_assignment=True a normal assignment would re-run the after-validator and recurse. If the team prefers normal attribute assignment, a guard would be needed instead. No other changes were made. app/service.py was not in scope for these findings and was not edited."}
